<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Progressive Liberal Party</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1953">1953</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Progressive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liberal-party-uk">Liberal Party</a> is a political party of the Bahamas, founded in 1953 to challenge the white merchant elite known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bay-street-boys">Bay Street Boys</a>. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lynden-pindling">Lynden Pindling</a> it won majority rule in 1967 and led the colony to independence in 1973, governing until 1992. It has since returned to power several times and remains one of the country's two main parties.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The party was founded on 23 November 1953 by William Cartwright, Cyril Stevenson and Henry Milton Taylor, and was the first national political party in the Bahamas. Earlier that year Cartwright and Stevenson had travelled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> to cover the coronation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elizabeth-ii">Elizabeth II</a>, meeting members of the British Labour Party there, and had visited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica-labour-party">Jamaica Labour Party</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-national-party">People's National Party</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jamaica">Jamaica</a>. The party opposed racial discrimination, corruption and poor administration under a restricted, property-based franchise, and called for wider voting rights, including votes for women. In 1956 it won about a third of the vote and six seats, forming the first organised opposition bloc in the House of Assembly.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lynden-pindling">Lynden Pindling</a>, who had joined a few months after the founding, became its parliamentary leader. In 1962 the party won the largest share of the vote but fewer seats, which it blamed on unfair constituency boundaries. On 27 April 1965, later called Black Tuesday, Pindling threw the Speaker's mace out of a window of the House of Assembly in protest, and the party boycotted the House for almost nine months. In 1967 it won 18 of the 38 seats, tying the United Bahamian Party, and with the backing of Randol Fawkes and Alvin Braynen formed the first black-led government.</p><p>Early independence was the issue of the 1972 election, which the party won with 29 of 38 seats, and the Bahamas became independent on 10 July 1973. In government it favoured stronger state control of the economy, more Bahamian ownership of business and the replacement of foreign workers by Bahamians. In the 1980s a Commission of Inquiry into drug trafficking reported corruption in the police and at cabinet level, and the issue split the party: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hubert-ingraham">Hubert Ingraham</a>, dismissed by Pindling, later led the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-national-movement">Free National Movement</a>, which won 33 of 49 seats in 1992.</p><p>Pindling retired in 1997, after the party had won only six seats, and Perry Christie led it back to office in 2002. It lost in 2007, returned in 2012, and in 2017, amid corruption scandals and the delayed opening of the Baha Mar resort, fell to four seats. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-davis">Philip Davis</a> it won 32 of 39 seats in 2021, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covid-19-pandemic">COVID-19 pandemic</a>, and on 12 May 2026 won 33 of 41 constituencies, the first back-to-back victory by a Bahamian party since 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Progressive Liberal Party” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After winning majority rule in 1967, Lynden Pindling's <b>Progressive Liberal Party</b> led the Colony of the Bahamas to independence on 10 July 1973 as the Commonwealth of The Bahamas, a parliamentary monarchy with the British monarch as head of state.</blockquote><a href="/state/bahamas">Bahamas</a></li><li><blockquote>Internal self-government came in 1964, and majority rule followed in 1967 under Lynden Pindling's <b>Progressive Liberal Party</b>, after a long campaign by the black majority.</blockquote><a href="/state/colony-of-the-bahamas">Colony of the Bahamas</a></li></ul></section>
